            "body": "Tab alignment with conceal: should a Tab align to the displayed column?\r\n\r\nRight now a Tab aligns to the raw column, so concealed cells push the next tab stop out. This is the same on master and in this PR. It's visible with the usual markdown link conceal:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n- [Docs](https://neovim.io/doc)<Tab>Reference\r\n- Intro<Tab>Overview\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith `tabstop=8`, `conceallevel=2` and the link markup concealed by extmarks, master and this PR both render:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n- Docs                          Reference\r\n- Intro Overview\r\n```\r\n\r\n`Reference` starts at col 32, and `Overview` starts at col 8. The line shows `- Docs`, which is 6 cells wide, yet the Tab is sized as if the 25 concealed characters were still there. With extmark conceal, the result is the same with `wrap` or `nowrap`. I haven't checked treesitter conceal separately.\r\n\r\nIf the Tab used the displayed column, both lines would line up:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n- Docs  Reference\r\n- Intro Overview\r\n```\r\n\r\nI read the comment at `plines.c:389` (\"Tab alignment ignores conceal\") as intentional, to match existing behavior. My question is whether that should hold for extmark conceal now that the cells really take no space.\r\n\r\nI think it shouldn't, for these reasons:\r\n\r\n- The feature treats concealed cells as taking no space. A Tab is the one place they still do.\r\n- Tabs are how people line up columns in markdown tables and lists, so the misalignment is easy to notice.\r\n- `wrap` and `nowrap` should differ only in what happens past the window edge, not in where text on the same row lands.\r\n\r\nWhat do you think? In my PR (#40897) I tried displayed-column alignment, but only under `wrap`. `nowrap` still uses the raw column, which is probably a bug.",
